# Design Document

> The desktop app lives in `apps/desktop/`; paths below are relative to it. The public
> landing site (`apps/landing/`) is described in [LANDING.md](LANDING.md).

Controller-first desktop game launcher for macOS, Windows, and Linux (macOS Apple Silicon
first). Orbit is a modern alternative to Pegasus Frontend, inspired by PS5, Nintendo Switch,
and Apple TV.

Built with **Tauri 2**, **React**, **TypeScript**, and **SQLite**. All filesystem and
process logic lives in Rust; the frontend never launches games directly.

## Purpose

- Fullscreen, couch-friendly game library with cinematic screen transitions.
- Import games from Steam, Astris (`.nsp`/`.xci`), macOS apps, and manual paths.
- Track playtime, install size, and achievements locally.
- One restrained glass console look with a Rust-generated material texture and portable compositor surfaces. Built-in game themes bundle logos and heroes, including five additional Studio presets with their saved palettes and artwork (Devil May Cry 5 Violet, Cyberpunk 2077 Violet, GTA VI Blue Hour, Super Mario Sunset, and Zelda Ocarina Gold). Theme Studio searches SteamGridDB logos and offers backgrounds from configured game artwork providers. Retired platform and color presets resolve to Liquid Glass. Custom artwork selection is explicit. Missing artwork is reported by the picker. Existing custom files remain on disk; the interface uses Liquid Glass styling.

## Universal gaming: Phase 1

Extend the existing launcher with a local Windows executable workflow. The first slice
adds installed Wine detection, per-game isolated environments, a persistent typed Wine
launch profile, bounded diagnostics, cancellable setup and owned process tracking.
Native, Steam, and Astris paths retain their existing behavior.
Runtime adapters live separately from library providers; a manually imported Windows
game is `manual` / `windows`, with a `wine` launch configuration.

Implementation sequence and decisions: [RUNTIMES.md](RUNTIMES.md). Managed setup
now downloads and verifies the pinned macOS Wine distribution inside Orbit, behind
a native macOS system flag. CrossOver bottles, D3DMetal/DXVK/DXMT provisioning,
installers and community profiles remain separately tested slices, not implied by
a working Wine launch. Apple GPTK requires its authenticated package/license flow;
the app must not pretend an unauthenticated download is available. Epic, GOG, and
streaming stay deferred. Windows Steam on macOS is specified in
[STEAM_WINDOWS.md](STEAM_WINDOWS.md). The first slice signs in through Steam's
login window, snapshots the owned library, downloads one Windows depot at a
time, and plays through the compatibility runtime.

Commands: `windows_compatibility`, `install_windows_compatibility`,
`orbit_wine_status`, `install_orbit_wine` (onboarding's Orbit Wine and its base container),
`detect_runtimes`, `register_wine_runtime`, `runtime_diagnostics`,
`component_views`, `set_component_view`,
`cancel_game_launch`; existing `create_game`, `update_game`, `launch_game` and
`stop_game` carry the rest of the workflow. Settings gains Compatibility; Add game →
Single file supports `.exe`; Edit properties exposes compatibility controls.

## Functional requirements

- User can browse a **home** screen with hero artwork and recent-game rows.
- User can browse, filter, and sort the **library** grid.
- User can open **game details** with playtime, size, achievements, and launch action.
- User can **add games** via importer scans or manual file pick (Add game journey).
- User can **edit properties** — title, description, portrait/landscape/square artwork (cached files or small inline refs in SQLite).
  Each artwork slot offers **Browse images** (a grid of results from SteamGridDB, TheGamesDB, RAWG, Wikimedia and the Steam Store, filterable by source) and **Random SteamGridDB** (applies a random SteamGridDB image for the matched game, avoiding the previous roll). Picks only apply to the draft until Save.
- Every imported game ends up with artwork in all slots: enrichment runs for new games and for existing games still missing real art; anything still empty gets a reused slot or a generated placeholder that stays eligible for later enrichment. Importer titles are corrected from a matched SteamGridDB / TheGamesDB / Steam Store entry (exact normalized title or Steam app ID only).
- User can **launch** a game; Orbit tracks the session and playtime.
- User can **stop** an active game session from the UI.
- User can sync **Steam** library metadata (playtime, SizeOnDisk, achievements) locally.
- User can **enrich** newly imported Steam games with store descriptions and SteamGridDB artwork (optional API key); existing games via Details → Settings → Refresh metadata. Refresh pulls Steam store copy first (then TheGamesDB) and never writes importer stubs such as leftover shortcut install notes.
- User can switch **themes** including custom JSON themes from Application Support.
- User can navigate entirely with a **gamepad** or keyboard fallback.

## Non-functional requirements

- **Local-first:** SQLite is the source of truth; no cloud sync in v1.
- **Controller-first:** spatial focus system; all primary flows reachable without a mouse.
- **Desktop-first:** macOS Apple Silicon remains the primary release target, with Windows and Linux install paths; fullscreen window management via Tauri.
- **Safe launch:** Rust validates paths and only launches resolved executables from known importers.
- **Performance:** avoid unbounded library loads; batch Tauri invokes where possible.

## Out of scope

ROM downloads/decryption, keys management, emulator distribution, an Orbit account,
cloud Steam login, Steam DRM bypass, and social features. Windows games run in
Orbit-owned Wine containers with a lightweight native Steam client for catalog,
downloads, and cloud file lists; games still authenticate through Valve's client
inside the container. See [STEAM_WINDOWS.md](STEAM_WINDOWS.md) and
[CONTAINERS.md](CONTAINERS.md).
Bulk “enrich entire library” jobs and Switch title-ID databases are deferred past 0.2.

