Library, Search, Home, and Details share one `LibraryEntry` identity. Owned Windows games through a local official Steam client are specified in [STEAM_WINDOWS.md](STEAM_WINDOWS.md). Sign-in opens Valve's Windows Steam client from Settings, then Orbit closes that Wine process and keeps the login. The Downloads sidebar lists in-progress transfers. That login is saved in `steam-windows` so later Play can seed each game container. Play clones an Orbit-owned Wine container, copies Steam files, and starts the game EXE with `SteamAppId` without waiting for silent `steam.exe`. See [STEAM_WINDOWS.md](STEAM_WINDOWS.md) and [CONTAINERS.md](CONTAINERS.md).

`open_steam_library()` opens Steam's library. `install_steam_game(appId)` validates the ID and checks Steam Store platform metadata before opening Steam's installation dialog.

### Theme artwork commands

`search_theme_artwork(query, slot)` searches SteamGridDB (`logo` or `hero`) and returns image dimensions. Theme Studio also searches connected game artwork providers for backgrounds. `fetch_theme_artwork(url, slot)` restricts downloads to approved image CDNs and validates the decoded raster before returning a data URL. No upscaling or cover fallback is used for theme backgrounds.

`get_hero_lighting(gameId, slot)` samples locally cached hero or cover art and returns three RGB bytes, or null if a safe local sample is unavailable.

`get_glass_material()` returns one cached, Rust-generated PNG grain tile and bounded blur settings for the shared glass surfaces. Native OS window materials vary by platform. Panels retain shared webview contents and provide opaque accessibility and unsupported-compositor fallbacks.

All translucent UI surfaces share the Liquid Glass material, including controls, fields, dialogs, session panels, and connected-game cards. Backspace edits text and has no navigation binding. Global navigation yields to text editing and IME composition.

`prepare_theme_previews(onPreview)` prepares the thirteen bundled game themes on a blocking worker. Native GPU resizing uses Metal, DX12, or Vulkan with a CPU fallback. Each completed theme streams small cached preview paths through a Tauri channel. Theme cards reserve a consistent frame with a loading shimmer and preserve the original artwork for theme selection.

Bundled themes ship optimized WebP display images and previews. Theme downloads and saves apply bounded image optimization on a worker while preserving transparency, aspect ratio, existing compact lossy files, and opaque bundle references.

Controller and keyboard navigation share a visible focus ring on every registered
control, including portaled dialogs. Pointer activity dismisses the controller ring.
The ring is inset so scroll containers cannot clip it.

`list_custom_themes()` reads and parses a bounded catalog on a worker. Embedded
artwork is materialized into cached display images and small previews before IPC;
returned themes carry optional runtime-only `preview` paths. Portable theme JSON
is preserved. Opening Themes reuses the hydrated catalog; the reload action reads
external edits explicitly.

### Settings redesign
Settings separates app preferences, appearance, library, default game graphics, and compatibility downloads. Graphics edits remain a session draft across navigation until explicit Save or Discard; only saved values affect launches. Download progress reflects native events and distinguishes preparation, transfer, verification/install, completion and failure. Version discovery does not imply install capability.

Settings refinement: restore the shared LiquidGlass content surface and use grouped icon-led rows. The compatibility catalog combines DXVK, DXMT and GPTK releases into one mutually exclusive graphics-engine version browser; it does not install or activate a backend. Saved graphics defaults continue to select exactly one backend.

## Interface standardization

The desktop retains Liquid Glass and shares Orbit-styled shadcn primitives. Library
view controls precede provider sections; Orbit hides connected catalogs. Initial
loads use layout-matched skeletons and background refresh preserves loaded games.
Game details prioritize game artwork and keep launch cancellation in the primary
action slot. Settings owns General, Artwork, and Launch & Compatibility.

Steam settings support additional existing Steam library folders, including
`steamapps/common`. Rust validates and normalizes the selected folders, persists
these preferences, and includes their manifests when detecting and launching
installed Windows games. This does not change new-download destinations.
Settings native controls participate in controller focus and show the shared ring.

Windows game executable discovery checks shallower directories first within its
entry/depth caps, so large asset/crash directories cannot hide a root executable.
Steam-linked Windows imports use manifest AppIDs for SteamGridDB square grids;
verified exact-AppID square artwork replaces legacy automatic icon fallbacks when
enriching/reimporting. Title-search results are not substituted for a known AppID.


## Unified library redesign (November 2026)

Library, Search, Home, and Details use a Rust-owned title identity (`steam:<AppID>`
for verified Steam titles, local UUID otherwise). Installation records retain their
game IDs, launch configuration, history and paths. One title has one card, regardless
of account ownership or installation count. Installed is the initial Library view;
All games share search, filters and sorting. The Favorites tab is omitted on Home
and Library until at least one title is marked favorite. Page size is bounded.

User-selected Steam library roots, steamapps and common directories are read
directly using bounded manifest discovery. Discovery needs neither account login
nor a compatibility runtime. Play links those installs into the managed Wine Steam
`steamapps` and registers the library with the client so Steamworks sees titles
such as God of War as installed. Legacy Steam-linked Windows records migrate to
Steam. A one-time SQLite cleanup drops leftover shortcut-provider rows and clears
engine paths that pointed into a retired third-party Wine folder, after a
recoverable snapshot. Installed files are never changed by migration. Steam
Windows titles always play through Orbit's own silent official Steam client.

Details contains Overview and Settings. Settings owns General, Artwork, and
Launch & compatibility editing; no separate Properties navigation. Global Settings
uses a controller-first sidebar with General, Themes, Library, Steam, Game graphics,
and Compatibility, plus a right-hand insights column. Metadata keys stay on General.
Shared glass surfaces and 48px controls use consistent focus, contrast and
reduced-motion fallbacks. Expandables use the shared Collapsible panel, not native
`<details>`. Library merge replaces importer placeholders (including leftover
shortcut install notes) with Steam store descriptions and can-you-run requirements. Catalog hydrate also
covers extra-library AppIDs that are installed on disk even when they are missing
from the owned snapshot, and overlayed store copy is written back onto those game
rows.

Metadata uses exact Steam identity whenever available, source provenance and persistent
user overrides. Cached library content renders immediately; background enrichment
prioritizes installed titles and covers the remaining catalog in bounded batches.

Homepage previews use Recent and Installed, plus Favorites only when the library has
at least one favorite (up to 12 games each, queried independently). Card activation
opens entry details; hero actions own launch and stop. No separate Steam shelf is
shown. Hero titles fit a single line within the existing content column.

The homepage carousel spans the viewport without horizontal padding. Artwork
fades at a screen border only while that edge overflows; a short shelf stays
fully visible, inset with the hero gutter, with no footer dissolve mask. Looping
shelves dissolve into the footer instead of clipping. The hero and header keep
their existing gutters.

Homepage shelves loop continuously in both directions when more than seven games
are shown, with bounded DOM copies and decoded artwork caching. Shorter shelves
stay a finite strip. Rebases keep an in-flight scroll moving forward so wrapping
a cycle does not snap backward. Focused cards ease inward from the screen edges
instead of jumping.

Windows launch preparation fails at the graphics stage when the selected backend
cannot be configured; it never silently substitutes Wine defaults. D3DMetal uses
the selected Apple payload's paired Windows and Unix libraries, with 64-bit PE
libraries installed only in system32 and the matching bridge path supplied to
Wine. Runtime compatibility and Steamworks client requirements remain separate
from successful graphics preparation.

A process spawn is not proof of successful game startup. If a launched session exits with a nonzero code, Orbit restores a dismissible failure banner for that game; normal exits and user stops do not restore it.

Steam sign-in uses an Orbit modal host and Valve's own Wine browser window. Readiness requires successful native placement, rather than merely spawning a process. The Wine window remains a separate OS window; it is not embedded web content. Only the managed Steam browser may be moved or hidden, and Cancel waits for native shutdown.

Steam sign-in must not fail because Wine is already stopped. Native shutdown uses
`wineserver -w` to confirm exit after a kill request, with bounded waits. Cancel
always releases the Orbit modal; an unexpected cleanup failure is surfaced outside
the dialog rather than trapping the user.

Steam library refresh reuses the official client's existing authenticated web
session. The backend reads only the managed client's Steam-domain cookie for the
selected account, unlocks it through Windows DPAPI/AES-GCM inside the same Wine
prefix, and calls the existing GetOwnedGames API. Credentials remain in transient
native memory; the frontend and logs never receive them. Sign-in completion waits
for the client web session before closing the client. A failed refresh preserves
the last successful per-account catalog. Local partial IDs cannot erase it.

Owned-library commands return the saved catalog before optional metadata work.
One background job hydrates details; an account check and indexed metadata merge
prevent it from restoring removed ownership or another account's games. Native
update events refresh every catalog view without replacing visible games with a
loading overlay.

Windows Steam games run beside the official authenticated client in Orbit's
managed Steam prefix. Existing user-selected Steam libraries are registered with
that client before launch. Steam's license checks and consent dialogs remain
Valve's responsibility. The game process, rather than the resident Steam client,
defines session lifetime. Stop shuts down only Orbit's managed prefix. Metal
backends require a compatible Wine build and matching Windows/Unix libraries;
Orbit must never silently substitute Wine's limited basic renderer.
